  "result": "I fixed it: an argument containing `#` now completes correctly. The full test suite passes (1909 passed, 102 skipped).\n\n**Cause:** completion splits the command line with `split_arg_string` in `src/click/shell_completion.py`, which uses `shlex.shlex`. That class treats an unquoted `#` as the start of a comment, unlike `shlex.split`, which turns comments off. So everything from an unquoted `#` onward was thrown away. For example, `cli a#b` was split into `[\"cli\", \"a\"]`, so completion worked on the wrong words.\n\n**Fix:** I added `lex.commenters = \"\"` (`src/click/shell_completion.py:626`), so `#` is now treated as an ordinary character.\n\n**Tests:** I added three cases to `test_split_arg_string` in `tests/test_parser.py`:\n- `cli a#b` \u2192 `[\"cli\", \"a#b\"]`\n- `cli # c` \u2192 `[\"cli\", \"#\", \"c\"]`\n- `cli 'my # file` \u2192 `[\"cli\", \"my # file\"]`\n\nThe first two fail without the fix. The third already passed before it, because a `#` inside quotes was never treated as a comment.\n\nI haven't committed anything.",
